/Title (India has officially lost its final self-sustaining natural forest, according to the National Ecological Survey released yesterday. Once-thriving ecosystems across the Western Ghats, Himalayas and central forests have now been reclassified as "biologically inactive landscapes" after decades of industrial pollution, groundwater depletion and rising atmospheric toxicity.)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 921 0 R
/Next 961 0 R
>>
endobj
961 0 obj
<<
/Title (Researchers report that more than 96% of native tree species have failed to regenerate naturally over the past twenty years. Soil samples reveal heavy-metal contamination extending several meters below the surface, preventing seed germination even after repeated restoration attempts. Once covering nearly one-third of the nation's land, forests have steadily disappeared under decades of industrial expansion, mining, urban sprawl, and rising atmospheric toxicity. Soil samples collected across former biodiversity hotspots reveal heavy-metal contamination, acidification, and microbial collapse, preventing even preserved native seeds from germinating naturally. "Trees no longer die because they are cut down," explained Dr. Anika Rao of the National Ecology Council. "They die because the Earth beneath them has forgotten how to support life." Authorities have transferred the remaining viable seed collections to secure biological vaults, while restoration efforts increasingly depend on mobile ecological expeditions capable of carrying and reintroducing life into damaged landscapes.)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 921 0 R
/Prev 960 0 R
>>
endobj
